# netsurf: MacSurf's CSS rewrites, 3-5x faster, and cached
# Generated 2026-09-24
#
# Hardware run 16 measured the rewrite chain (patch 76) at 12.6 s for
# claude.ai's 1.4 MB sheet, 3.7 s for YouTube's 437 KB, 1.3 s for
# Wikipedia's 169 KB -- and 362 ms for the same 42 KB of inline CSS on
# every reformat. Timed per pass on a desktop, two passes were 76%:
# - modern_compat's declaration drops made one full scan per property name
#   (43); now one scan tries every name, and only at word starts whose
#   letter begins some name. A blanked declaration holds no name, so the
#   result is the same.
# - logical_properties tried all 30 aliases at every byte; now only at
#   word starts beginning b, i, m or p.
# - Every pass now runs only if one of its trigger names occurs in the text
#   (found with memchr), via a same-signature wrapper round each pass.
# Output is byte-identical on claude.ai's, YouTube's and Wikipedia's
# sheets; claude.ai's goes 382 -> 143 ms on the desktop.
# css.c keeps the last 16 rewrites by content hash, so re-parsed <style>
# blocks cost nothing.

